                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c366f610-726f-3cf1-8a15-24e2b2f7b9a9",
                        "entry_id": "69335723-cd80-3450-9394-897e8c90c29f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Initialism of Garden of Eden."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "abbreviation",
                            "alt-of",
                            "initialism"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "cec8d259-7406-4fb5-ab44-1e244c89193b",
                                "sense_id": "c366f610-726f-3cf1-8a15-24e2b2f7b9a9",
                                "text": "About a year and a half ago, Schroeppel and Hickerson found a bunch of patterns with very small predecessor counts. These are used as modules in existence proofs for GOE's within various rectangles, some quite cozy."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b0f109a2-3c63-4ca8-a1c6-a5dd3139688a",
                                "sense_id": "c366f610-726f-3cf1-8a15-24e2b2f7b9a9",
                                "text": "This describes briefly the discovery of the first GOL GOE by Roger Banks, saying: \"[he] used sophisticated mathematical techniques to prove that a certain 9-by-33 rectangular pattern is itself a Garden-of-Eden pattern.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4bf92d4d-b179-4874-8f86-129b1d556f1c",
                                "sense_id": "c366f610-726f-3cf1-8a15-24e2b2f7b9a9",
                                "text": "As far as I know, even a definite result for the 6x6 case would be progress. The most recent investigation of GoEs that I know about was some interesting work that Donald Knuth recently did in passing for his upcoming Volume 4 of _The Art of Computer Programming_, using binary decision diagrams."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
